# OPOS — Company OS Framework

OPOS is an AI-native company operating system expressed as a GitHub repo of markdown and JSON. It encodes your company (mission, policies, knowledge), your departments (charters, data, processes), your roles (agents), and your work-in-flight (backlogs) using Claude Code's native cascading primitives. No runtime, no orchestration code — just templates and folder conventions you fork and adapt.

OPOS is distributed as a **Copier template**: scaffold a new company instance with one command, then receive upstream updates via the agent-driven `sync-from-core` skill or the opt-in GitHub Actions workflow.

## Scaffold a new company instance

**Prerequisites:**
- Python 3.10+
- `copier` ≥9.0.0 — install via `pip install copier` (or `pipx install copier`)
- `git`
- `gh` CLI authenticated against your GitHub account (`gh auth login`)
- A GitHub account with read access to `Koroqe/OPOS`

**Scaffold:**
```bash
copier copy --vcs-ref v0.5.0 gh:Koroqe/OPOS my-company-os -d COMPANY_NAME="My Company"
cd my-company-os
git init && git add -A && git commit -m "chore: initial OPOS scaffold (v0.5.0)"
gh repo create my-company-os --private --source=. --push
```

That gives you a fresh OPOS instance with all CORE files (agents, skills, templates) pre-installed and {% raw %}`{{ COMPANY_NAME }}`{% endraw %} substituted to your company name. The STARTER files (example departments, configs) ship once for you to customize — `copier update` will never overwrite them. **As of v0.5.0**, root `CLAUDE.md` is also consumer-owned post-scaffold (so your edits to Mission and Values survive future `copier update` runs).

## First steps after scaffold

After `copier copy` finishes, your repo is structurally complete but content-blank in six places: Mission, Values, strategic priorities, dept missions (the 6 v0.5.1 default depts: rnd, finance, people, legal, commercial, pr), and the policies folder. Run the `company-setup` skill (owned by `coo`) to fill these in conversationally:

1. Open the repo in your editor (VS Code recommended for the inline Claude Code session).
2. Open a Claude Code session at the repo root.
3. Invoke `/company-setup`. ~10 questions; ~15 minutes total. Progress is written to files as you go.

When complete, browse your populated OS via `/serve-console` (opens `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`). The skill will print the exact next-step commands at the end.

If you re-run `/company-setup` on a populated repo it will refuse — to re-do, restore the `<one short sentence>` placeholder in root `CLAUDE.md`'s Mission section first.

## How to use OPOS day-to-day

After `/company-setup`, open Claude Code at the repo root and **talk to your `chief-of-staff`** — the OPOS steward (NEW in v0.5.2). The steward is the single conversational entry point. You state goals; the steward decomposes and executes:

- **"Let's ship a new feature for [thing]"** → steward proposes a slice plan, runs `task-register`, paces through commits, runs `release-from-changelog`, closes the issue.
- **"Audit our company state"** → steward reads `.claude/.current-task`, recent history, open issues; reports a 1-page state summary.
- **"We need a marketing analyst"** → steward routes through `people-lead` → `allocate-resource` → AI-first decision tree → either a `/design-agent` recommendation OR a job spec written to `company/hiring/`.
- **"We need a customer-success department"** → steward routes through `ops-manager` → `design-department` (NEW v0.5.3) → consultations with `ceo` + `coo`, charter drafted from `DEPARTMENT.md.tmpl`, lead-agent designed in the same session via a `/design-agent` recommendation, files written under `departments/customer-success/`.
- **"Should we open a sales office in Berlin next quarter?"** → steward routes through `coo` → `deliberate-decision` (NEW v0.6.1) → 2-round consultation with all 6 dept-leads + ceo → arbiter renders APPROVE / REJECT / DEFER with rationale → decision artifact written to `company/decisions/`. **Cost note:** ~15 consult-agent-pattern Task calls per deliberation; trigger only for decisions whose stakes justify the cost (strategic direction, hiring, market entry — NOT routine tactical choices).
- **"Let me work on these two features in parallel — open one terminal for each"** *(NEW v0.7.0)* → steward in terminal A invokes `/task-register` for feature A → issue #X opened, `.current-task` now contains `X`. Steward in terminal B invokes `/task-register` for feature B → issue #Y opened, `.current-task` now contains `X` then `Y` on separate lines. Each terminal's steward sees BOTH tasks in its First-touch greeting; routine `task-update` / `task-complete` calls auto-pick when 1 active and require `--issue` when multiple. `task-pause` / `task-resume` work per-issue. **Parallel sessions are first-class as of v0.7.0** — the pre-v0.7.0 single-active-task guard that REFUSED a second `task-register` is gone.
- **"We need a compliance sub-dept under legal"** *(NEW v0.8.0)* → steward routes through `ops-manager` → `/design-subdept --parent legal --description "regulatory compliance + policy attestation"` → consultations with `legal-lead` + `coo`, charter drafted from new `SUBDEPT.md.tmpl` with sub-dept escalation chain (sub-lead → legal-lead → coo → ceo), `/design-agent` recommended for the `compliance-lead` agent in the same session, files written under `departments/legal/compliance/`. Closes the LAST org-chart-shape gap: ops-manager now owns the complete quartet (design-process + design-agent + design-department + design-subdept).
- **"What's the deploy status?"** → steward consults `eng-lead` via `consult-agent` and summarizes.

The steward executes autonomously by default for low-risk operations (reads, tests, drafts). It asks one-line confirmation for `git commit`, `gh issue close`, agent creation. It asks explicit approval for `git push`, release cutting, destructive operations. See [`.claude/agents/company/chief-of-staff.md`](.claude/agents/company/chief-of-staff.md) "Permission tiers" section for the full ladder (5 levels: Auto, Notice, Confirm, Explicit approval, Hard refuse).

When you open Claude Code at the repo root, the steward greets you with current state in ≤3 lines (active task, paused tasks, last activity) and asks "What can I do?" — no setup prompts, no menus. The greeting is skipped automatically for ad-hoc lookups ("show me task #6" gets an immediate answer, not a status report).

You don't memorize skill names. You don't know template paths. You state intent — the steward maps it to capability.

## Scheduling processes

*(NEW v0.6.0)* Any process can fire on a cron schedule via Claude Code's built-in cron tools (`CronCreate` / `CronList` / `CronDelete`), wrapped by OPOS's three scheduling skills (`/schedule-process`, `/unschedule-process`, `/list-scheduled-processes`). This is what turns OPOS from request-driven to autonomous-capable: weekly metrics reports, daily standup nudges, monthly financial summaries — they run without your laptop being open.

**Prerequisite:** you must be logged into Claude Code (`claude login`). The built-in cron tools authenticate against your Claude Code subscription. No additional OPOS setup is required.

### Opt-in via four PROCESS.md frontmatter fields

Add these to any `PROCESS.md` to make the process schedulable. All four are required together (validator rejects partial sets):

```yaml
schedule: "0 9 * * 1"        # 5-field POSIX cron. Rejects '* * * * *' (every-minute).
runtime: claude-schedule     # v1: only `claude-schedule` allowed. v2 may add gha / launchd.
non_interactive: true        # Literal boolean true. Scheduled runs have no human to answer prompts.
authority:                   # Allow-list of actions the runner may take without human review.
  - write_proposal           # Allowed: commit, push, open_pr, file_issue, comment_issue, write_proposal, read_only.
                             # read_only is mutually exclusive with all other values.
```

### Three skills

| Skill | Wraps | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `/schedule-process <name>` | `CronCreate` | Reads PROCESS.md, validates frontmatter, composes a routine prompt with an authority prelude, registers the routine, caches the routine id locally. Idempotent (no-op on re-run with no diff; confirms on cron change). |
| `/unschedule-process <name>` | `CronDelete` | Cancels the live routine. Leaves PROCESS.md frontmatter untouched so you can re-activate without re-editing. Idempotent (no-op if not scheduled). |
| `/list-scheduled-processes` | `CronList` | Read-only drift detection. Classifies each row as OK / MISSING / ORPHAN / DRIFT / INVALID_INTENT. CronList is authoritative — safe on fresh machines. Warns on overlapping cron times (conflict risk if processes mutate shared files). |

### Design-time integration

When you run `/design-process`, the steward presents 4 trigger-mechanism options (was 3 before v0.6.0): manual / hook-driven / hybrid / **scheduled**. Picking "scheduled" populates the four frontmatter fields above and eager-creates the `scheduled-runs/` folder.

### Authority is a declared contract, NOT a runtime sandbox

v1 enforces `authority:` by prompt injection + in-band self-check: the scheduled routine's prompt begins with a prelude reading *"You are running as a scheduled routine. Your declared authority is [...]. Before taking any action, verify it is in this list; refuse and write the refusal to the scheduled-runs/ entry if not."* A misbehaving prompt body could in principle take actions beyond the declared list — there is no post-run sandbox guard in v1 (that's a v2 work item; see RISKS Risk 18). Mitigation: every `scheduled-runs/` entry starts as `verification_state: unverified` until a human reviews the output and marks it `verified`.

### Run records live in `scheduled-runs/`, not `history/`

Scheduled invocations write to `<skill>/scheduled-runs/` (sibling to history/, never mixed). Manual interactive invocations of the same process still write to history/. Split keeps daily-cron noise from drowning out the rare manual process-improvement signal. The SKILL body distinguishes by checking the invoking prompt for the prelude string `"You are running as a scheduled routine"`.

### Example narrative (10 use cases condensed)

A typical user journey: (1) design a weekly metrics rollup via `/design-process`, pick "scheduled" trigger at step 7, cron `0 9 * * 1`, authority `[write_proposal]`. (2) `/schedule-process weekly-metrics-rollup` registers the routine; (3) Monday at 09:00 the runtime fires, the process writes a markdown proposal under `scheduled-runs/`; (4) you review it Monday morning and mark `verification_state: verified`. Other journeys: typo in cron `"every monday"` → validator rejects with format spec; missing one of the 4 fields → validator rejects with the missing list; cron registration succeeds but cache write fails → wrapper auto-rolls-back via `CronDelete` (or surfaces the orphan id for manual cleanup); two processes declared at same cron `0 9 * * 1` → `/list-scheduled-processes` warns about file-conflict risk; fresh machine joins the project → `/list-scheduled-processes` correctly reports all live routines as OK (cache is a hint, not authoritative); routine cron drifts (edited via Claude Code's native UI) → `/list-scheduled-processes` reports DRIFT, re-`/schedule-process` reconciles.

## Updating from upstream

Three ways to receive updates from `Koroqe/OPOS` — **pick exactly one sync driver per repo** (they compete otherwise; `auto-sync` refuses to run when the Action's schedule is enabled):

1. **Autonomous (v0.9.0; durable since v0.10 — RECOMMENDED).** Register `auto-sync`: `/schedule-process auto-sync`. It ships with `runtime: cloud` — each fire spawns an isolated Claude Code session in Anthropic's cloud with its own checkout, so it keeps running with your laptop closed, **billed to your Claude subscription: no Anthropic API key, no API billing**. One-time prerequisite: connect your GitHub account to claude.ai (the Claude GitHub App) so the cloud session can reach your repo; runs and logs appear at https://claude.ai/code/routines. Two alternatives exist for the same job: `runtime: gha` (a hardened GitHub Actions workflow — durable too, but a CI runner can't use your subscription login, so it needs an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret and usage-based API billing) and `runtime: claude-schedule` (session-scoped — fires only while a Claude Code session is open; same-machine testing, not autonomy). Clean updates are applied and committed with no human action; conflicts leave a work branch and file a GitHub issue in your repo. New releases soak for `min_release_age_hours` (default 24) before any consumer applies them — the maintainer's own instance is the canary.
   **Rollback runbook:** one consumer → `git revert <sync-commit-sha>` (the sha is in the run record), optionally re-pin via `/sync-from-core --target_version <old-tag>`. Fleet-wide bad release → the maintainer deletes/yanks the GitHub release; every consumer's next probe then simply no-ops on it (the probe reads live releases), and a follow-up release ships the fix.
2. **Agent-driven, human-reviewed (DEFAULT for new consumers).** `check-for-updates` is invoked automatically as step 1 of `task-register`, `task-update`, `task-complete`. When the upstream has a newer release, you see a one-line notice in the skill output. Run `/sync-from-core` when you're ready to apply and review the diff yourself.
3. **Opt-in GitHub Actions (background PRs).** Edit `.github/workflows/sync-opos.yml` and uncomment the `schedule:` block to enable weekly auto-PRs (Mon 3am UTC). Runs on GitHub's servers; your laptop is uninvolved.

See [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) Risks 9–13 for known limitations (manual conflict resolution, rollback, breaking-change migration) and Risks 31–34 for the v0.9.0 loop.

## The self-improvement loop (v0.9.0)

OPOS is bidirectionally self-improving across every company that runs it, through three pure Claude Code mechanisms:

- **PULL** — `auto-sync` (above): every consumer picks up each upstream release autonomously.
- **PUSH** — every process run already records a `proposed_delta` (see "Self-improvement log schema"). The weekly `review-history` routine (owner: `coo`) triages the open ones: fixes to consumer-owned (STARTER) files are applied locally or drafted to the owning dept's backlog; defects in framework (CORE) files are routed to `propose-to-core` (owner: `chief-of-staff`), which drafts a **generic, fully anonymized** fix and opens a PR on the upstream repo. CORE files are never edited locally — that is what keeps your future syncs conflict-free.
- **CLOSE** — the upstream maintainer reviews and merges `[opos-core]` PRs and cuts a release; every consumer's next `auto-sync` pulls the fix. A mistake observed in ONE company becomes a fix shipped to ALL of them.

**Anonymization.** Before anything leaves your machine, the proposal passes a three-layer redaction gate: a deterministic blocklist-grep + secret-regex pre-gate, an adversarial `redaction-reviewer` agent pass (fail-closed: anything but a literal `REDACTION: PASS` blocks the send), and — on any failure — a local draft for human review instead of a PR. The PR content (diff, title, body, branch, commit message, commit identity) carries no company-identifying data and no secrets. **Disclosure:** your GitHub *account* is still visible as the PR author (and fork owner, for non-maintainer consumers — forks go to the user account, never a company org). Use a neutral GitHub account for `gh` if that matters to you.

**Scheduled-run authority exception (governance).** Interactive sessions follow the chief-of-staff permission tiers unchanged. For scheduled routines, the human authorization happens **once, at `/schedule-process` registration** (itself a Confirm-tier action): every action inside the process's declared `authority:` list is thereby pre-authorized, including the branch-then-ff-merge integration to the default branch that `auto-sync` and `review-history` perform. Registration is the review-and-authorize moment; the run records (with `verification_state: unverified`) are the audit trail you review after the fact.

**Permissions for non-interactive runs.** A cron-fired session cannot answer permission prompts. At registration, `/schedule-process` proposes the minimal `.claude/settings.json` allow entries the process's declared authority needs (narrowly scoped — e.g. `Bash(git push origin:*)`, never blanket `gh api`), and you confirm them. Nothing is pre-authorized in the scaffold default.

## Quickstart (legacy — no longer needed; Copier handles substitution)

1. Clone this repo (or fork it). _Note: prefer `copier copy` above; this manual flow is for framework developers._
2. Substitute the framework tokens for your company. List every remaining token with:
   ```bash
   grep -rn "<<[A-Z_]*>>" . --include="*.md"
   ```
   The primary token is {% raw %}`{{ COMPANY_NAME }}`{% endraw %}. Once substituted, this command should return zero matches.
3. Pick one department to start with. The `departments/rnd/` folder is a worked example with both research and engineering content (engineering folded in as the building branch at v0.5.1) — use it as the reference for adapting another dept's structure.
4. Add real MCP servers in `.mcp.json` and configure per-agent access via each agent's `tools:` allow-list.
5. Open Claude Code at the repo root and verify the smoke test (see [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) for the recipe).

## The cascade model

Claude Code automatically walks the directory tree from your session's working directory upward, loading every `CLAUDE.md` it finds. A session opened in `departments/rnd/` sees:

```
departments/rnd/
        ↑ (closer files load last, highest priority)
departments/rnd/CLAUDE.md           ← dept charter (R&D umbrella; eng-lead + eng-reviewer report up)
        ↑
company/CLAUDE.md                   ← if cascade passes through it
        ↑
CLAUDE.md                           ← root constitution
```

Skills (`.claude/skills/<name>/`) nest similarly. Subagents (`.claude/agents/**/*.md`) are scanned recursively from the repo root.

## Where things live

| Primitive | Nests by folder? | Location | Notes |
|-----------|------------------|----------|-------|
| `CLAUDE.md` | YES — auto-cascades up the directory tree | any folder | Use for constitution + dept charters |
| Skills (`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`) | YES — nested `.claude/skills/` work | repo root or any dept folder | Each dept can host its own skills |
| Subagents (`.claude/agents/**/*.md`) | YES — recursive scan; subdir is purely organizational; identity = `name` frontmatter | `.claude/agents/<dept>/<role>.md` | Subfolders for visual grouping |
| Slash commands | Merged into skills per docs; prefer skills | n/a | `.claude/commands/X.md` and `.claude/skills/X/SKILL.md` are equivalent |
| Settings (`.claude/settings.json`) | NO — project root only | `.claude/settings.json` | Per-dept settings are not supported |
| MCP (`.mcp.json`) | Root-only | repo root | Per-agent access via `tools:` allow-list |

